  "primary_source": {
    "path": "runs/20261006-companion-priority/smoothed-hessian-lower-preread/source-primary.sphmc-v1.raw.html",
    "raw_sha256": "ec485cdad5fe140114be35eef93d19398e0cafcf21abb3fff1bdbbf462e5f94d",
    "lf_sha256": "ec485cdad5fe140114be35eef93d19398e0cafcf21abb3fff1bdbbf462e5f94d",
    "raw_bytes": 1858750,
    "access": "Existing immutable pinned literal v1 HTML, independently reread; metadata/old approval not substituted for source"
  },
  "primary_anchors": [
    {
      "anchor_id": "S1.p1.1",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S1.p1.1",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 55508,
      "char_end": 56956,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "f75aa384eb98ace175b61ecde0369c677d4f6f9e80ab40808c292540dbfc631a",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 1467,
      "literal_text": "We study the problem of sampling from a target probability density π ∝ exp ⁡ ( − V ) \\pi\\propto\\exp(-V) over ℝ d \\mathbb{R}^{d} , where V ∈ C 2 ​ ( ℝ d ) V\\in C^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^{d}) satisfies, for 0 &lt; α ⩽ β 0&lt;\\alpha\\leqslant\\beta ,"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S1.E1",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S1.E1",
      "tag": "table",
      "char_start": 56957,
      "char_end": 58440,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "1cb6ad517e12a49253464a96c67403a25e6252160438bf2ad7354473daf4f80e",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 1506,
      "literal_text": "α ​ I ⪯ ∇ 2 V ​ ( x ) ⪯ β ​ I , x ∈ ℝ d , κ ≔ β α . \\alpha I\\preceq\\nabla^{2}V(x)\\preceq\\beta I\\,,\\qquad x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}\\,,\\qquad\\kappa\\coloneqq\\frac{\\beta}{\\alpha}\\,. (1.1)"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S2.p1.1",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S2.p1.1",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 99299,
      "char_end": 101442,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "21a3a2dc263725d211232048eb131531a1039238540a4a9cfd50beacfa9346f6",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 2179,
      "literal_text": "Except in the generic warm start result Theorem 6.1 and its consequence Theorem 7.1 , throughout the remainder of the paper V ∈ C 2 ​ ( ℝ d ) V\\in C^{2}(\\mathbb{R}^{d}) and ( 1.1 ) holds pointwise. For most of the paper, we normalize β = 1 \\beta=1 , so that κ − 1 ​ I ⪯ ∇ 2 V ⪯ I \\kappa^{-1}I\\preceq\\nabla^{2}V\\preceq I ; this can be achieved by rescaling."
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S2.E1",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S2.E1",
      "tag": "table",
      "char_start": 102317,
      "char_end": 104057,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "d47a8246d0901e2dec57a00a05e14bda7a811b37cd0148a77d77717650419719",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 1760,
      "literal_text": "prox a ​ V ⁡ ( y ) ≔ argmin x ∈ ℝ d { V ⁡ ( x ) + ∥ x − y ∥ 2 2 ​ a } . \\operatorname{prox}_{aV}(y)\\coloneqq\\operatorname*{argmin}_{x\\in\\mathbb{R}^{d}}{\\Bigl\\{V(x)+\\frac{\\lVert x-y\\rVert^{2}}{2a}\\Bigr\\}}\\,. (2.1)"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S2.Ex1",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S2.Ex1",
      "tag": "table",
      "char_start": 105667,
      "char_end": 107244,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "e2d4954f608d621384062233109155f31edee85a7f8a0828077c8734e1561a48",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 1603,
      "literal_text": "R a , y ​ ( d ​ x ) ∝ exp ⁡ { − V ⁡ ( x ) − ∥ x − y ∥ 2 2 ​ a } ​ d ​ x . R_{a,y}(\\mathrm{d}x)\\propto\\exp\\Bigl\\{-V(x)-\\frac{\\lVert x-y\\rVert^{2}}{2a}\\Bigr\\}\\,\\mathrm{d}x\\,."
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S2.p2.2",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S2.p2.2",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 104058,
      "char_end": 105477,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "c9d9622440f00faebd3b9be07cc44c47413a046cfe4c8bef789d03779375b386",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 1428,
      "literal_text": "In this paper, we only ever need to call the proximal oracle for a β \\beta -smooth potential with step size at most 1 / ( 2 ​ β ) 1/(2\\beta) . In this regime, the computation of prox a ​ V \\operatorname{prox}_{aV} is a well-conditioned optimization problem and can be easily implemented via standard routines. In Appendix D , we remove the use of proximal queries altogether."
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.SS1.p1.3",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1.p1.3",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 252153,
      "char_end": 252997,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "13ce4657d77e8d89de8795418164523b58a6d79fa05b6c1c2ab7a6003eb27f51",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 850,
      "literal_text": "Let X | Y = y X\\mid Y=y be drawn from R η , y R_{\\eta,y} . Recalling the definition ( 2.1 ) of the proximal operator, throughout this section we write"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.Ex3",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex3",
      "tag": "table",
      "char_start": 252998,
      "char_end": 253874,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "1107894881c1a6a104f7bf4361164f11d456f9626aaf8214aa3c131e7ae37adf",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 883,
      "literal_text": "x y + ≔ prox η ​ V ⁡ ( y ) . x_{y}^{+}\\coloneqq\\operatorname{prox}_{\\eta V}(y)\\,."
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Thmtheorem1.p1.1",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 256933,
      "char_end": 257342,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "a8e60df5284254fd8bb16d1cd4da2a65e7d9061d6d0bc2aba4e0b77d0f4c8269",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 412,
      "literal_text": "For 0 &lt; η ⩽ 1 0&lt;\\eta\\leqslant 1 ,"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.SS1.p4.1",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1.p4.1",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 273629,
      "char_end": 274417,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "a6b7f7ae1cec2ee808e359e811238d94adb7435c393527da1aec0d9e40a1c997",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 795,
      "literal_text": "Proximal optimality gives ∇ V ​ ( x y + ) = ( y − x y + ) / η \\nabla V(x_{y}^{+})=(y-x_{y}^{+})/\\eta . Define"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.Ex8",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.Ex8",
      "tag": "table",
      "char_start": 274418,
      "char_end": 276158,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "23d12fb9b403c0d49a54342dc2fa78cf6771b89ad9fc94d867f3d3c33d4a02be",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 1767,
      "literal_text": "ρ y ​ ( u ) ≔ V ⁡ ( x y + + η ​ u ) − V ⁡ ( x y + ) − η ​ ⟨ ∇ V ​ ( x y + ) , u ⟩ . \\rho_{y}(u)\\coloneqq V(x_{y}^{+}+\\sqrt{\\eta}u)-V(x_{y}^{+})-\\sqrt{\\eta}\\,\\langle\\nabla V(x_{y}^{+}),u\\rangle\\,."
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.SS1.p4.2",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1.p4.2",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 276159,
      "char_end": 278348,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "4c61d3646e7e8c09e3a92ce53b2e01bde97f3469323a440543abe255ecc0a1fd",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 2217,
      "literal_text": "Then 0 ⪯ ∇ 2 ρ y ⪯ η ​ I 0\\preceq\\nabla^{2}\\rho_{y}\\preceq\\eta I , and under R η , y R_{\\eta,y} , ( X − x y + ) / η (X-x_{y}^{+})/\\sqrt{\\eta} has density proportional to u ↦ e − ∥ u ∥ 2 / 2 − ρ y ( u ) u\\mapsto e^{-\\lVert u\\rVert^{2}/2-\\rho_{y}(u)} . Call this law 𝗋 y \\mathsf{r}_{y} . We have"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.E6",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.E6",
      "tag": "table",
      "char_start": 278349,
      "char_end": 280682,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "601c07c36f592ec891b13b22cb0574c21a0c6df7e60c7b4fe9d8139c3c9cf822",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 2384,
      "literal_text": "W 2 ​ ( 𝗋 y , 𝖭 ⁡ ( 0 , I ) ) ⩽ 𝔼 𝗋 y ​ [ ∥ ∇ ρ y ​ ( U ) ∥ 2 ] ⩽ η ​ 𝔼 𝗋 y ​ ∥ U ∥ 2 ⩽ η ​ d . W_{2}(\\mathsf{r}_{y},\\mathsf{N}(0,I))\\leqslant\\sqrt{\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathsf{r}_{y}}[\\lVert\\nabla\\rho_{y}(U)\\rVert^{2}]}\\leqslant\\eta\\sqrt{\\mathbb{E}_{\\mathsf{r}_{y}}\\lVert U\\rVert^{2}}\\leqslant\\eta\\sqrt{d}\\,. (4.6)"
    },
    {
      "anchor_id": "S4.SS1.p4.3",
      "url": "https://arxiv.org/html/2609.06906v1#S4.SS1.p4.3",
      "tag": "p",
      "char_start": 280683,
      "char_end": 282539,
      "raw_utf8_outer_span_sha256": "31e5b0a24c6d8b3055713cd2c48ae293a859cc209b5df82150162f4b9a471fa6",
      "raw_utf8_bytes": 1870,
      "literal_text": "Indeed, the first inequality combines Talagrand’s inequality for the standard Gaussian with the Gaussian log-Sobolev inequality; the second uses that ∇ ρ y ​ ( 0 ) = 0 \\nabla\\rho_{y}(0)=0 and ∇ ρ y \\nabla\\rho_{y} is η \\eta -Lipschitz; and the last follows by integration by parts since 𝗋 y \\mathsf{r}_{y} is 1 1 -strongly log-concave with its mode at 0 0 ."
    }
  ],
